WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.788
When you buy a bicycle, you pay its price, and maybe a delivery charge to get it home. Later you pay to repair a puncture and to insure it. The first costs get you the bike. The later costs keep it running.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:32.345
Businesses sort costs in the same way. Capital expenditure buys a non-current asset, gets it ready for use, or improves it. Revenue expenditure runs the business and keeps assets working.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:48.971
For each item, ask: does it buy, prepare or improve a non-current asset? If yes, it is capital. If it is a cost of using, running or repairing, it is revenue. Tick one column only.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:01:04.768
Watch for pairs. The first sign on a new van is capital, but repainting it later is revenue. Legal fees on buying premises are capital, but loan interest is revenue. An extension is capital, a repair is revenue.

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:12.630
If totals are asked, list the capital items and add them, then do the same for revenue. Show your working.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:26.509
Here is a real question from June 2024. Tick whether each item is capital or revenue expenditure, when buying a motor vehicle. Five marks, one for each row.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:37.039
The cost of the vehicle buys a non-current asset, so it is capital. The delivery cost gets the vehicle to the business, ready to use, so it is capital too.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:45.764
The extended warranty pays for repair cover in future years, while the vehicle is being used. That is a running cost, so it is revenue.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:57.247
Fuel is used up running the vehicle, and insurance is a regular running cost. Both are revenue. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:09.195
A common mistake is treating delivery or installation as revenue. These costs get a new asset ready for use, so they are capital.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:19.650
Another is treating insurance or fuel as capital because it was paid on the same day as the van. The date does not matter. They are running costs.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:32.332
Now you try. Lee is opening a café. Decide whether each cost is capital or revenue, then total each group. Pause and decide.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:41.266
The machine, its installation and the new shelving are capital. The repair, the loan interest and the coffee beans are revenue.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:51.609
Capital: three thousand plus two hundred plus four hundred, three thousand six hundred dollars. Revenue: eighty plus one hundred and fifty plus five hundred, seven hundred and thirty dollars.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:03:05.934
Remember: if it buys, prepares or improves a non-current asset, it is capital. If it runs or repairs, it is revenue. Tick one column, then total each group. Now try the practice questions.
